## 🗳️ California 2026 Ballot Guide: Your Complete Voter's Pamphlet

**Voting Day:** Tuesday, November 3, 2026 · **14 propositions** (5 legislative, 9 citizen initiatives) · **$798.9M spent** — the most expensive ballot-measure cycle in California history. The single biggest story is the **billionaire-tax three-way fight** (Props 40, 41, 42), where the measure with the most "Yes" votes prevails if they conflict.

---

## 💰 The Big Money Measures (Bonds & Taxes)

The four bond and tax measures together commit roughly **$44 billion in borrowing** plus permanent tax decisions that shape the state's budget for decades.

### 🏠 Proposition 1 — Veterans & Affordable Housing Bond

**Legislative Statute** · Costs taxpayers **$500M–$600M/year for ~25 years** · Polling: **55% Yes / 43% No**

Authorizes **$11.25 billion** in bonds. The split matters:

| Portion | Amount | Repaid By |
|---|---|---|
| General affordable housing | **$10B** | All taxpayers (General Fund) |
| CalVet Home Loan Program | **$1.25B** | Veterans' mortgage payments (self-supporting, **zero taxpayer cost**) |

The $10B covers multifamily rentals (≥10% for extremely low-income households), supportive housing, down-payment assistance, farmworker/student/tribal housing, and preservation of existing affordable units. It's expected to support up to **40,000 rental units and 40,000 homeownership households**.

- **The debate:** Opponents say the "Veterans" title is misleading since only ~11% goes to veterans. Supporters counter it unlocks federal matching funds and addresses 40,000 units "in limbo" from prior funding gaps.

### 💎 Proposition 2 — Rainy Day Fund Expansion

**Constitutional Amendment** · No new tax (reallocates existing revenue) · Polling: **60% Yes / 39% No**

Doubles the cap on California's Budget Stabilization Account from **10% to 20%** of General Fund tax revenue, and — the most consequential change — **exempts deposits from the Gann Limit** (the state spending cap). Currently, saving money counts as "spending," which discourages the state from building reserves in boom years.

- **Who wins:** Budget stability, public employees (fewer furlough risks), students (less mid-year cut exposure).
- **The trade-off:** Taxpayers get **fewer and smaller rebates** in good years because more is saved.
- Also extends mandatory debt repayment (pensions, retiree health) to **2039–40** and allows using set-aside funds to pay down the state's **$20B federal unemployment insurance loan**.

### 📈 Proposition 3 — High-Income Tax Made Permanent

**Constitutional Amendment** · No new tax (extends existing) · Polling: **58% Yes / 40% No**

Makes the top marginal rates (10.3%, 11.3%, 12.3%) **permanent** instead of letting them sunset in 2031. Affects single filers earning **$371,000+** and joint filers **$742,000+** — roughly the **top 2%** of taxpayers.

- **Where money goes:** ~89% to K-12 schools, ~11% to community colleges; also sustains up to $2B/year for Medi-Cal.
- **The stakes:** If it fails, the state loses **$5B–$15B/year** starting in 2031.
- **Key nuance:** This is *not* a new tax — voters approved it in 2012 and extended it in 2016. Writing it into the **constitution** (vs. statute) makes it much harder to reverse. One caveat: research shows behavioral responses (relocation, income restructuring) eroded ~45% of the original tax's expected revenue in its first year.

### 🏡 Proposition 37 — Middle-Income Homebuyer Loans

**Initiative Statute** · **No direct state cost** (private lenders bear default risk) · Polling: **61% Yes / 35% No**

Authorizes CalHFA to issue up to **$25 billion** in revenue bonds for a "Middle-Class Homeownership Loan" program.

| Feature | Detail |
|---|---|
| Loan size | Up to **17% of home price** (second mortgage) |
| Buyer's down payment | At least **3%** |
| Home type | **Newly constructed** only (or converted non-residential) |
| Income cap | Up to **200% of Area Median Income** |
| Residency | 1+ year in California |
| Default risk | **Private lenders**, not taxpayers |

In Los Angeles County, a family of four earning up to **~$216,200** qualifies. Unlike other programs, there's **no first-time-buyer requirement** and **no equity clawback**. The 17% + 3% structure means buyers hit the 20% down-payment threshold that avoids mortgage insurance.

### 🧬 Proposition 38 — Immunology Research Bonds

**Initiative Statute** · Bond repayment from General Fund · Polling: **55% Yes / 42% No**

Authorizes **$8.4 billion** in bonds for immunology research (cancer, Alzheimer's, heart disease).

- **Split:** 50% to a new UC-affiliated research institute; 50% to competitive grants.
- **Built-in returns:** Drugs developed must be **discounted 20%** for Californians, and **10% of drug revenue** flows back to the state — designed to make the bonds partially self-funding.
- **Timeline:** Research outcomes may take **10–20 years**; this is a long-term investment.

## ⚖️ The Billionaire Tax Showdown (Props 40, 41, 42)

⚠️ **This is the story of the election.** These three measures are **scored against each other**: if all pass, the one with the **most "Yes" votes prevails** where they conflict. Together they've drawn about **$360M in spending** — roughly 45% of the entire cycle.

### 💸 Proposition 40 — One-Time 5% Wealth Tax

**Constitutional Amendment & Statute** · Polling: **52% Yes / 46% No**

A **one-time 5% tax** on the net worth of individuals over **$1 billion** who were California residents on **Jan 1, 2026**, with net worth measured **Dec 31, 2026**. Estimated **200–250** people pay. Revenue is estimated at **tens of billions** (Hoover/Stanford estimates center around $28–36B).

| Detail | Value |
|---|---|
| Payment due | 2027 (can spread over **5 years with interest**) |
| Revenue split | **90% healthcare** (Medi-Cal, mental health, substance use) · **10% education & food assistance** |
| Ongoing risk | Possible **< $1B/year** loss in income tax if billionaires leave |

**What counts (and doesn't) as net worth:**

| ✅ Included | ❌ Excluded |
|---|---|
| Publicly traded securities (Dec 31, 2026 price) | Primary residence |
| Private business stakes (book value + 7.5× avg profits × ownership %) | Pensions, 401(k)s, IRAs (Roth IRAs exempt up to $10M) |
| Art, IP, vehicles (up to $5M excluded) | Tangible property outside CA for 270+ days |
| Grantor trust assets; dependents' assets >$50K | Directly held real estate |

Anti-avoidance rules: gifts over $1M made after **Oct 15, 2025** are added back; trusts funded in 2026 count 100%, 2025 trusts 75%.

**The money trail:** Opponents spent **$99.6M** vs. supporters' **$32.2M** (as of Oct 6). Top donors for "No" include **Sergey Brin ($88.5M+)**, Eric Schmidt ($40.2M), L. John Doerr ($40M), Patrick Collison ($33.2M). **Gov. Newsom officially opposes** Prop 40, arguing it risks revenue loss. Stanford economists warn departures could cost $1–3.5B/year — a net future loss of ~**$38.9B** in today's dollars, they argue.

### 🔍 Proposition 41 — Tax Audits & Spending Limit

**Constitutional Amendment** · Polling: **51% Yes / 44% No**

Two provisions:
1. **Bars any new state tax** (enacted on/after Jan 1, 2026) from being excluded from the **Gann spending limit**.
2. **Requires the State Auditor** to review programs funded by new special taxes **before** they reach the ballot (triggered at 25% of signatures collected) and **every 4 years** after, with audits required to identify **at least 10% annual cost-reduction opportunities**.

**Why it targets Prop 40:** Prop 40 explicitly exempts itself from the spending limit. **If Prop 41 gets more "Yes" votes than Prop 40, that exemption is voided** — effectively neutralizing the wealth tax's revenue structure.

### 🛡️ Proposition 42 — Ban on Property, Retirement & Retroactive Taxes

**Constitutional Amendment** · Polling: **54% Yes / 43% No**

Permanently prohibits the state from imposing:
- New taxes on **personal property** (cars, boats, jewelry — real estate is already capped by Prop 13)
- New taxes on **retirement income** (pensions, 401(k)s, IRAs, annuities)
- New taxes on **savings** (interest, dividends, capital gains)
- Any **retroactive tax** (taxing income earned before enactment)

**Why it matters for Prop 40:** If Prop 42 gets more "Yes" votes than Prop 40, it could kill the wealth tax if the wealth tax is construed as a tax on property or savings. Critics call 41 and 42 "deceptive measures" put on the ballot by a small group of billionaires to cancel the wealth tax; supporters frame them as genuine fiscal-accountability and taxpayer-protection measures.

