## 🏛️ Governance, Voting & Local Government

### 🪪 Proposition 39 — Voter ID Requirement

**Constitutional Amendment** · Polling: **43% Yes / 55% No** · Closest funding parity ($39.5M for / $33.3M against)

Requires voters to present **government-issued ID**. For mail-in ballots, voters must write the **last four digits** of their designated ID on the return envelope — ballots that don't match state records are **invalidated**.

- **Does NOT** require proof of citizenship at registration, and does **NOT** restrict mail-in voting (unlike the federal SAVE Act).
- **Pro side:** "Common-sense reform" to prevent non-citizens from voting; requires an independent audit every two years.
- **Con side:** Adds new hurdles, creates identity-theft risk (writing ID numbers on envelopes), and disproportionately affects low-income voters, people with disabilities, and recent movers. One analysis estimates it would require **26M+ existing voters to re-register** to designate an ID number.
- **Cost:** Tens of millions to low hundreds of millions per year.

### 🏛️ Proposition 43 — Local Special Tax Two-Thirds Threshold

**Legislative Constitutional Amendment** · Polling: **43% Yes / 51% No**

Raises the voter-approval threshold for **citizen-proposed local special taxes** from a simple majority to **two-thirds (66⅔%)**, effective **Jan 1, 2027**.

- **Scope:** All local governments — cities, counties, school districts, community college districts, special districts.
- **Does NOT affect** general taxes (still simple majority) or taxes proposed by local governments themselves.
- **Context:** This **restores** the two-thirds requirement that existed before 2017 court rulings (the *Upland* decision) allowed a simple majority. It was a **compromise** between the Legislature and the Howard Jarvis Taxpayers Association to pull a more restrictive tax proposal off the ballot.
- **The debate:** Taxpayer groups say it protects against new local taxes; opponents say it gives outsized power to a minority and makes it harder to fund local services.

### 🩺 Proposition 44 — Health Clinic 90% Patient-Care Spending

**Initiative Statute** · Polling: **34% Yes / 61% No** (most unpopular measure)

Requires private nonprofit **Federally Qualified Health Centers (FQHCs)** to spend at least **90% of annual revenue** on "program services" (direct patient care). The **Attorney General** defines what qualifies.

- **Penalty:** If a clinic falls short, it pays the state the **shortfall amount**. It can **earn the money back** if it meets the standard within **5 years**; otherwise the state keeps it for clinic workforce programs.
- **Other fines:** $5,000, then $10,000/month for late reports.
- **Excluded:** Tribal and urban Indian clinics.
- **The debate:** Seven clinics funding the "No" campaign reported Mission Spend Ratios from **64.5% to 83.9%**. Supporters (originated by SEIU-UHW) say it ends executive bloat; opponents say it threatens the financial viability of safety-net clinics serving ~2,000 sites statewide.

### 🔄 Proposition 5 — Recall Election Reform

**Legislative Statute** · Polling: **35% Yes / 59% No** (second-most unpopular)

**Removes the successor election** from the recall ballot. Currently, voters vote to recall *and* choose a replacement in the same election — and a successor can win with just a **plurality**.

| Scenario | If Prop 5 Passes |
|---|---|
| Governor recalled in **first 2 years** | Special election consolidated with the next statewide primary |
| Governor recalled **after 2 years** | Lieutenant Governor finishes the term |
| Other statewide offices (AG, SOS) | Governor **appoints** a replacement |
| Legislators | Separate special election |

The recalled official is **banned from being appointed** but **can run** in a subsequent special election. **Fiscal impact:** Net unknown — savings or costs of millions depending on the office.

### 🌿 Proposition 45 — CEQA Reform ("Building an Affordable California Act")

**Initiative Statute** · Polling: **49% Yes / 46% No** (most contested)

Creates a **voluntary expedited CEQA track** for nine categories of "essential projects" (housing, water, clean energy, transportation, wildfire mitigation, broadband, education, medical facilities, public safety). The track is **opt-in** for applicants but **mandatory for agencies** once chosen.

| Change | Detail |
|---|---|
| Final EIR deadline | **365 days** |
| Application completeness | **30 days** |
| Negative declaration | **180 days** |
| Public comment | **45 days** (draft EIR) / **20 days** (negative declaration) |
| Project alternatives | Only **1** (plus "no project") |
| Court standard | **"Substantial evidence"** (harder to win than "fair argument") |
| Litigation window | Challenge filed within **30 days**; decided within **270 days** |
| Project voiding | Court **cannot void the entire project** — only the affected portion |

**Excluded from the fast track:** Nuclear power, new oil & gas, high-speed rail, detention facilities, Delta conveyance.

- **Who benefits:** Developers, homebuyers (faster housing = potentially lower prices), clean-energy and wildfire-mitigation projects.
- **Who's concerned:** Environmental groups — less review time, fewer alternatives, harder to litigate. A live debate is whether **data centers** qualify as "broadband infrastructure" (supporters say no; opponents say the authors would have excluded them explicitly if intended).
- **Cost:** High tens of millions to **$100M+/year** for local governments to implement.

### 📢 Proposition 4 — Public Campaign Financing

**Legislative Statute** · Polling: **42% Yes / 53% No**

**Repeals the 1988 ban** on public funding of campaigns (Government Code §85300), allowing **any** state or local government to create a public-finance program. Key guardrails:

- **Permissive, not mandatory** — no government must create a program.
- Funds **cannot** come from education, transportation, or public-safety budgets.
- Candidates must show "strict criteria" of broad-based small-dollar support.
- **Barred uses:** Legal defense, fines, repaying personal campaign loans.
- Programs **cannot** discriminate by party or incumbent status.
- **Triples** maximum fines for illegal foreign campaign contributions.

15 states + DC already have some form of public financing; SF and LA already have programs as charter cities. This extends the *option* to all other jurisdictions.

