## 🎯 What This Means for Your Vote

- **If you prioritize fiscal safety nets:** Props 1, 2, 3, 37, 38 all lean "Yes" on stability.
- **If you prioritize tax restraint:** Props 41, 42, 43 lean "Yes."
- **The Prop 40/41/42 cluster is the pivotal decision** — your vote across all three determines whether the billionaire tax survives.
- **Props 5 and 44 are the most unpopular** and likely to fail; **Props 37 and 2 are the most popular.**

## 📌 Limitations & Unknowns

- **Prop 40 revenue** depends on net worth as of Dec 31, 2026 — estimates range widely ($28–36B per Hoover/Stanford) and could swing with markets.
- **Prop 45** long-term fiscal effects are uncertain; the $100M+/year figure is initial implementation cost.
- **Prop 41/42 vs. Prop 40 conflict resolution** hinges on the *most Yes votes* rule; the exact legal mechanics of how a "conflict" is determined (e.g., is the wealth tax a "property tax" under Prop 42?) remain legally unsettled and could end up in court.
- For full legal text, consult the **California Secretary of State Voter Guide** at voterguide.sos.ca.gov.

